{"attack": true,"case": "The target's own mechanism refutes its central claim. It frames the preference as 'a bet on the currently fittest survivor,' and justifies choosing the better-corroborated theory 'because it has proven itself the most robust instrument for survival and problem-solving so far.' A 'bet' is inherently forward-looking: it is an action taken on the expectation of a future payoff. To justify that bet by citing past performance ('proven itself... so far') is the precise structure of inductive reasoning — using observed past regularity as the rational basis for expecting future success. The target cannot escape this by retreating to 'action, not belief,' because a bet that is rational requires a reason to expect the bet to pay off, and the only reason offered is past survival. This directly triggers the target's own countercondition: 'If the preference implies any increase in subjective probability or rational expectation of future success, it collapses into induction.' The evolutionary analogy fails to rescue the argument because natural selection is a retrospective explanatory mechanism (it explains why current survivors exist), whereas the scientist's preference is a prospective choice. Organisms do not 'prefer' fitness — they merely survive. Conflating retrospective selection with prospective choice smuggles induction in through the very concept of a 'bet.'","premise": "A rational 'bet' on a theory's future problem-solving efficacy, justified by that theory's past performance, constitutes an inductive inference from past success to expected future success — regardless of whether the bet is labeled 'action' rather than 'belief.'","premise_evidence": [{"block": "6b403926ebe9a3929dc93dcc46ef85c1f6e5e02d75db8d0087b262316dac20d6","quote": "we prefer the theory that has withstood the most severe tests not because it is more likely true, but because it has proven itself the most robust instrument for survival and problem-solving so far."},{"block": "6b403926ebe9a3929dc93dcc46ef85c1f6e5e02d75db8d0087b262316dac20d6","quote": "The 'preference' is a bet on the currently fittest survivor, acknowledging the risk of future refutation."}],"successor_question": "Can a forward-looking rational choice be justified by past performance without any inductive commitment, or does the very act of citing past survival as a reason for present preference already presuppose that the past is relevant to the future?"}